WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.301
Parameter count describes one dimension of model size, but architecture and implementation also matter.

00:00:06.301 --> 00:00:15.449
More parameters usually require more memory and compute, yet a larger model is not automatically better for every task or runtime.

00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:21.750
Quantization reduces numerical precision to lower memory use and often improve practical inference speed.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.832
Lower precision can affect output quality differently across models and tasks.

00:00:26.832 --> 00:00:32.320
Test the actual quantization rather than assuming one label guarantees a result.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.808
G G U F is a common model-file format used by llama.

00:00:37.808 --> 00:00:39.231
cpp-compatible runtimes.

00:00:39.231 --> 00:00:48.379
A file name may describe architecture and quantization, but compatibility also depends on runtime support, tokenizer, prompt template, and model metadata.

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:55.900
The context window is the number of tokens the system can consider in a request and conversation.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:01.794
Longer context increases memory use, prompt-processing time, and the chance of irrelevant information.

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:06.063
Advertised maximum context is not a default operating target.

00:01:06.063 --> 00:01:10.738
During generation, the key-value cache stores attention information for context.

00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:15.820
Cache size grows with model design, context, concurrency, precision, and runtime.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:22.935
A model that fits at startup may run out of memory during longer or simultaneous requests.

00:01:22.935 --> 00:01:29.236
Fit planning includes weights, cache, runtime overhead, display use, operating-system load, and safety margin.

00:01:29.236 --> 00:01:40.009
When the whole model does not fit on the accelerator, partial GPU offload can place some work on the GPU and some on the CPU.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:43.872
Measure speed and stability under the intended workload.
